00:20:19,970 --> 00:20:23,250
 Newton's plagiarized my work on the differential calculus here.

250
00:20:23,730 --> 00:20:27,910
 Anyway, this theory of gravitation is clearly completely absurd.

251
00:20:28,430 --> 00:20:29,890
 Your opinion on it, Mr. Huygens?

252
00:20:30,350 --> 00:20:30,870
 Mm-hmm.

253
00:20:31,570 --> 00:20:34,300
 They treated him like that, and they were great.

254
00:20:34,750 --> 00:20:37,590
 Hook, Huygens, Leibniz.

255
00:20:37,810 --> 00:20:39,410
 Yes, yes, yes, yes.

256
00:20:39,640 --> 00:20:41,350
 And what did Isaac Newton do?

257
00:20:42,320 --> 00:20:44,180
 Oh, Newton, well, he bore it all.

258
00:20:45,220 --> 00:20:47,440
 As usual, he was into a thousand things.

259
00:20:47,540 --> 00:20:49,840
 Wind resistance, projectiles, impacts.

260
00:20:51,940 --> 00:20:53,620
 Politics and pendulums.

261
00:20:54,960 --> 00:20:56,440
 How sound is propagated.

262
00:20:56,440 --> 00:20:58,220
 The properties of liquids.

263
00:21:01,120 --> 00:21:06,380
 There's a thousand things, like meteorology, theology, chemistry, alchemy.

264
00:21:06,500 --> 00:21:07,860
 Oh, yes, yes, alchemy.

265
00:21:17,850 --> 00:21:57,130
 Indeed, he was very much into alchemy, and he was interested in money, too.

266
00:21:57,450 --> 00:22:00,070
 He even became director of the Mint in London.

267
00:22:00,370 --> 00:22:03,010
 His feats in outwitting forgers became legendary.

268
00:22:10,570 --> 00:22:20,800
 Newton, you've been elected president of the Royal Society.

269
00:22:26,130 --> 00:22:32,040
 Protocol is quite strict, right me. Now, go ahead.

270
00:22:40,510 --> 00:22:59,210
 And more than anyone in advancing science, we are pleased to recognize your achievements.

271
00:23:00,010 --> 00:23:01,390
 Arise, Sir Isaac.

272
00:23:03,490 --> 00:23:10,560
 Everything Sir Isaac Newton taught us over three centuries ago, that's all still true.

273
00:23:10,640 --> 00:23:12,520
 And he made a great contribution to our knowledge.

274
00:23:12,720 --> 00:23:16,120
 His laws of universal mechanics opened the way to our modern technology.

275
00:23:16,120 --> 00:23:21,080
 And with pure mathematics, just constructs of the mind, pure intelligence.

276
00:23:21,800 --> 00:23:23,600
 Not bad, eh, for a lad from the farm.

277
00:23:23,760 --> 00:23:25,820
 Yeah, nobody's ever done any better.

278
00:23:26,540 --> 00:23:27,430
 Relativity?

279
00:23:27,550 --> 00:23:29,270
 Ah, well, yes, certainly, to be sure.

280
00:23:29,390 --> 00:23:31,290
 Einstein, well, there is Einstein, yes.

281
00:23:31,710 --> 00:23:34,100
 But he, too, in his head will remake our universe.

282
00:23:34,690 --> 00:23:35,810
 That's another story.

283
00:23:36,010 --> 00:23:36,710
 It's fascinating.

284
00:23:37,680 --> 00:23:38,980
 Tell you, don't worry.

